WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:09.160
So carbon is an extraordinarily important
element. The mass

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:14.730
of carbon 12 is used as the standard for measuring
the

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:18.340
weight of all the other atoms on the periodic
table.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.780
So carbon it’s around us everywhere, we
are all carbon, there

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.410
is lots of carbon in us, even more than the
hydrocarbons

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:28.109
within our body, lots of the structures inside
us and many of

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:31.000
the materials which we use every day. In fact
the majority of

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.199
us use carbon in the form of fuel in our cars
to get to work or

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:35.250
to go home.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:41.300
It is also, of course, the basis of all the
life, the so-called

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.420
organic compounds which make up you and me,
but also the

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.870
element carbon itself is also quite exciting.
And it exists in a

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:54.629
number of forms, for example here is amorphous
carbon,

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:58.030
that’s carbon that does not have any particular
form. This is

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.510
an element such as you would use in a gas
mask, the gas

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:06.150
goes in here, the poisonous material is absorbed
and clean air

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:09.400
comes through here so you can breathe in and
out through

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.400
there.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.780
So carbon is an extremely common element,
but it comes in

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.720
a number of different alatropes, so different
forms. And this

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.890
one here, this is actually some charcoal,
which is just simple

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.730
carbons, very, very similar in fashion to
graphite.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.950
It is also, I think that carbon is the element
which has

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.850
perhaps been the subject of one of the most
exciting

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.320
developments the whole time I have been a
chemist. When

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.230
Harry Kroto and his colleagues discovered
so-called

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:41.190
buckminster fullerene, this molecule C60 which
has the shape

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:45.750
of a football, which nobody had imagined would
exist and be

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:50.450
stable and now chemists all over the world
are using it and

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.801
not only can you get carbon arranged as footballs,
but they

00:01:53.801 --> 00:02:00.920
now realised that you can arrange them as
long tubes, so-

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:06.050
called nano-tubes, and you can even put the
C60 the football

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:09.539
inside the nano tube. And one of my young
colleagues here in

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:13.010
Nottingham is making what are called nano-peapods
where

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:17.420
you put these balls of C60 all the way along
a nano-tube just

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:18.970
like the peas in a pod

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.890
So carbon comes as graphite, as diamond, and
also as bucky-

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.249
balls or fullerenes. So the carbon here which
is a bit like the

00:02:26.249 --> 00:02:30.500
graphite is very amorphous and it’s very,
very free-flowing.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.379
So we are going to pour a little bit of the
carbon on to the

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:35.709
paper so you can see. So you can see it is
a very light

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:41.409
powder, a very dark powder and you can see
it is a very nice

00:02:41.409 --> 00:02:47.200
easily manoeuvrable, easily workable material,
very beautiful

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.999
material. Often used as a lubricant in the
form of a graphite

00:02:52.999 --> 00:02:57.329
which is very, very soft in one dimension
but also the other

00:02:57.329 --> 00:03:01.261
allotrope of carbon, which is diamond, is
extremely hard; one

00:03:01.261 --> 00:03:05.169
of the hardest materials known to us. Very,
very unreactive

00:03:05.169 --> 00:03:08.110
and perhaps it’s why we give it to our loved
ones as

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:09.099
engagement rings.

